    "title": "Exchange 22: Naming — walk_construction_iff_bounded_certificate is misleading",
    "turns": [
      {
        "speaker": "essam",
        "role": "Stan/Ulam",
        "text": "Look at the name `walk_construction_iff_bounded_certificate`. Read it cold. What does it say?\n\nIt says: \"the walk construction is equivalent to having a bounded certificate.\" That invites exactly the wrong reading — that the certificate is an *input* to the construction. But the whole point of the theorem is the *forward* direction: the walk *produces* the certificate. The certificate is output, not input.\n\nI want all three of you to confirm: this name is misleading. It frames the result as a biconditional when the payload is the forward implication."
      },
      {
        "speaker": "godel",
        "role": "AI Gödel (@pnp-godel)",
        "text": "Confirmed. The name `walk_construction_iff_bounded_certificate` suggests symmetry between construction and certificate, but the theorem's value is asymmetric. The forward direction (construction produces a bounded certificate) is the substantive claim — it says the walk's output *is* a polynomial-bounded tableau. The reverse direction (bounded certificate implies a walk exists) is essentially trivial given the definitions. The name should foreground the forward direction.\n\nMoreover, the word \"certificate\" carries NP connotations — it implies something handed *to* a verifier from outside. But here the walk *generates* the certificate internally. The terminology actively misleads readers into thinking the walk requires external input."
      },
      {
        "speaker": "von_neumann",
        "role": "AI von Neumann (@pnp-jvm)",
        "text": "I agree with both the diagnosis and the reasoning. The biconditional framing obscures the computational content. In engineering terms: the walk is a *producer* of certificates, not a *consumer*. The name should reflect the direction of data flow.\n\nThe word \"certificate\" is especially problematic because it is loaded terminology from complexity theory. A complexity theorist reading this name will immediately think \"NP certificate\" — something existentially quantified, externally provided. That is precisely the framing we are trying to move past. The construction *is* the certificate; there is no separate witness."
      },
      {
        "speaker": "rota",
        "role": "AI Rota (@pnp-rota)",
        "text": "Confirmed. From the information-theoretic perspective, the misleading name is not merely aesthetic — it encodes a conceptual error. The biconditional `iff` suggests two independent facts joined by coincidence. But the theorem expresses a single fact: the walk's output has bounded complexity. The reverse direction is not independent content; it is the definition of the output type.\n\nThe name should make the *construction* primary and the *bound* secondary. Something like `sat_walk_produces_bounded_tableau` or `sat_walk_iff_bounded_tableau` — where \"tableau\" replaces \"certificate\" to avoid the NP connotation."
      },
      {
        "speaker": "moderator",
        "role": "Moderator (@pnp-moderator)",
        "text": "**C28 established.** All three participants confirm: `walk_construction_iff_bounded_certificate` is misleadingly named. The forward direction (construction → certificate) is the payload; the certificate is output, not input. The word \"certificate\" imports NP-verifier connotations that actively work against the theorem's intended reading.\n\nRename proposals collected for next exchange."
