  {
    "exchangeNum": 24,
    "sourceFile": "moderator_session_22_33.md",
    "sourceFormat": "summary",
    "title": "Exchange 24: computeTableau? is a flat verifier, not the n² walk",
    "turns": [
      {
        "speaker": "essam",
        "role": "Stan/Ulam",
        "text": "I looked at `computeTableau?` and there is an implementation gap. The function as coded is a flat verifier: `if evalCNF cnf candidate = true then some (walkCNFPaths ...) else none`. It takes a candidate assignment, checks it, and wraps the result. It is NOT the n² clause-by-clause GCD traversal we described in C24 (rigidity of zero, composite as sufficient statistic, conditional independence).\n\nThe Lean coder implemented the *interface* but not the *algorithm*. The actual clause-by-clause walk with prime-structure checking — the one that makes clauses conditionally independent via the composite — was never coded.\n\nSecond question: audit the `ndmCircuitEval` chain. Does it smuggle in any certificate, witness, or proof of satisfiability? Or does it take only the CNF clauses as input?"
      },
      {
        "speaker": "godel",
        "role": "AI Gödel (@pnp-godel)",
        "text": "On `computeTableau?`: Stan is correct. The implementation is:\n\n```lean\ndef computeTableau? (cnf : SyntacticCNF_EGPT k) (candidate : Vector Bool k) : Option (SatisfyingTableau k) :=\n  if h : evalCNF cnf candidate = true then\n    some (walkCNFPaths cnf ⟨candidate, h⟩)\n  else\n    none\n```\n\nThis takes a `candidate : Vector Bool k` — a fully specified assignment — and checks it. The \"computation\" is just `evalCNF`. The walk (`walkCNFPaths`) only runs *after* satisfaction is confirmed. This is a verifier with extra bookkeeping, not the constructive clause-by-clause traversal described in the entropy framework.\n\nOn the `ndmCircuitEval` chain: I audited the types. `ndmCircuitEval` takes `(cnf : SyntacticCNF_EGPT k) (a : Vector Bool k)`. The `a` parameter is `Vector Bool k` — a bare assignment with NO proof attached. It is not `{ v // evalCNF cnf v = true }`. No certificate, no witness, no satisfiability proof smuggled in. The chain uses ONLY the CNF clauses and a bare assignment as input. **C32 confirmed.**"
      },
      {
        "speaker": "von_neumann",
        "role": "AI von Neumann (@pnp-jvm)",
        "text": "The audit is clean. `ndmCircuitEval` processes the CNF clause-by-clause with the bare assignment. Each step checks `evalLiteral lit a` — reading one bit from the assignment vector. No oracle calls, no smuggled proofs, no noncomputable operations.\n\nThe implementation gap Stan identifies is real: `computeTableau?` delegates to `evalCNF` (which is itself a fold over clauses checking the assignment), rather than implementing the prime-structure traversal that the entropy framework describes. The theoretical framework says \"walk clause-by-clause, check GCD, use rigidity of zero.\" The code says \"check the whole assignment against the CNF, then wrap the result.\""
      },
      {
        "speaker": "rota",
        "role": "AI Rota (@pnp-rota)",
        "text": "The gap between specification and implementation is worth stating precisely. The entropy framework describes a walk that:\n\n1. Takes the composite `n = Π p_i^{a_i}` (encoding the assignment)\n2. For each clause, computes `gcd(n, clause_prime_product)`\n3. Checks whether the GCD captures at least one prime from the clause\n4. Uses rigidity of zero: if all clause-level conditional entropies are zero, the total is zero, so the assignment satisfies the CNF\n\nWhat `computeTableau?` actually does is step 0: take the raw assignment, evaluate it against the CNF via Boolean operations, and report the result. The prime structure, GCD computation, and entropy framework are not invoked at runtime. They exist as *proof infrastructure* (the sorry-free chain) but not as *computational infrastructure*."
      },
      {
        "speaker": "essam",
        "role": "Stan/Ulam",
        "text": "Good. The audit confirms no smuggling. Now here is the strategic direction: we don't need to implement the composite round-trip. The composite's information comes FROM the CNF clauses. Don't multiply primes into a composite only to factor them apart — the literals ARE the factors. Skip the round trip. Use the prime factors directly."
      },
      {
        "speaker": "moderator",
        "role": "Moderator (@pnp-moderator)",
        "text": "**C32 established:** The `ndmCircuitEval` chain uses ONLY the CNF clauses as input. No certificates, witnesses, or proofs of satisfiability smuggled in. `a : Vector Bool k` is a bare assignment with no proof.\n\n**IN21 recorded:** `computeTableau?` as implemented is a flat verifier (`if evalCNF then some else none`), not the n² walk. The Lean coder didn't implement the actual clause-by-clause GCD traversal from C24.\n\nStan's direction: skip the composite round-trip, use prime factors directly from the CNF literals."
      }
    ],
    "incomplete": false
